  "query_preview": "/SUNDAY THROUGH WEDNESDAY/...\nIssued at 245 AM CDT Thu Aug 27 2026\n\nClimatologically abnormal heat returns into early next week:\n\nCome Saturday, the ridge currently over the southwest CONUS is \nforecast to begin building northeastward into our area, bringing\nwith it the passage of a surface warm front Saturday night and\nSunday morning. This will revitalize southerly flow and \nsubsequent compressional and advection heating. According to \nNAEFS and ECMWF ESATs, 99.5th percentile 700 mb temperatures \nwill result in above normal temperatures, with the Extreme \nForecast Index at values of 0.85-0.95. These values have been\nsteadily increasing the past few forecasts as global ensembles\ntighten toward the upper end of the temperature spreads. Highs \nare forecast to warm into the 90s, and even near 100 F, by early\nnext week. Heat Index values will also rise to 95-105 F, with \nthe greatest values toward the Missouri-Arkansas border. While \nthis level of heat is not crazy for what we've already \nexperienced this August, it is abnormal for early September (as \nhighlighted by the ESATs and EFI). The CPC Heat Index \nclimatology/forecast shows 90th percentile Heat Index values for\nearly September at 95-100 F, with a 90-99% chance to exceed \nthat range at some point in early September.\n\nIn addition to the abnormal heat for this time period, these \ntemperatures are expected to persist over multiple days, which\nwill bring back a Heat Risk for those vulnerable to heat and/or\nwithout proper cooling methods. NWS HeatRisk brings in Major to\nExtreme values to our area Tuesday and Wednesday. Heat-related\nprecautions will need to be taken as we enter this timeframe.\n\nWith ridging and high pressure in place, rain chances are\nforecast to be minimal into early next week. That said, global\nmodels and NBM rain chances are quite persistent within\nsouthwesterly flow just north of our area, so it would not be\ntoo out of place to see a few rain chances sneak into the area\nat some points.\n\n\nPotential for some elevated fire weather this weekend:\n\nWhen southerly flow returns behind the surface warm front\nSaturday, moisture is forecast to lag a bit behind.\nAdditionally, the winds are expected to be a bit breezy,\nespecially west of Highway 65, where sustained speeds of 10-20\nmph and gusts up to 15-25 mph are possible. Pair this with the\nhot temperatures in the 90s, and you have a recipe for hot, dry,\nand breezy conditions. The Hot, Dry, and Windy Index (HDWI) is\npicking up on this, with near certainty in above 90th percentile\nconditions for this time of year, and around a 50% chance of\nexceeding the 95th percentile. Therefore, some elevated fire\nweather concerns may become present west of Highway 65 where the\nhottest, driest, and breeziest conditions are currently\nforecast. This is especially true as this are is currently in a\nModerate Drought and continuing its way into Severe Drought \nterritory.",
